  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nThe deepest troughing pattern finally moves off to the east/ flattens\nout a bit and drier air moves into the northwest CWA with subtle\nridging. A moister airmass exists primarily along and southeast of \nI-71 along with a wedge of MLCAPE between 1500-2000 J/kg and low \nlevel lapse rates around 7.5C/km. This corridor is primarily where \nisolated thunderstorm chances will persist through the day today, \nthough expect coverage to be more limited compared to Monday. Once \nagain, storms will contain locally heavy rainfall and frequent \nlightning. Cannot rule out some isolated strong wind gusts. As an \naside, areas northwest of I-70 are forecast to have RHs in the 50s \nwhile areas south and east linger in the upper 60s to low 70s during \nthe afternoon on Tuesday. Definitely a moisture gradient in place \nright across the CWA. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nBy Wednesday evening, things finally start to shift a bit more with \nthe larger flow. The southerly troughing will lift north, bringing a \ndisorganized Gulf low up across the Tennessee Valley region and \nAppalachians. This system will clip our southerly counties along the \nOhio River and into north central KY/ central OH with renewed chances\nfor largely diurnal scattered thunderstorms Wednesday and Thursday \nafternoon.\n\nMeanwhile, to our north a much larger trough will be moving into\neastern Canada, dragging a long slow moving cold front down through\nthe Great Lakes region. This feature looks to impact our area \nThursday through Saturday, with showers and thunderstorms once again\nexpected in a very moist overall airmass. PWATs of 2 inches with the\npotential for training storms introduces a renewed flooding threat\nacross the region. WPC has place a Slight Risk ERO for Friday across\nour area from I-70 southward with similar thinking.",
